WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.018
Symmetric encryption uses the same secret key to protect and recover data.

2
00:00:05.018 --> 00:00:12.952
It is commonly used when an application needs confidentiality for stored data, message payloads, or protected values.

3
00:00:12.952 --> 00:00:21.836
The security of the result depends on the algorithm, mode, key, nonce or IV handling, metadata handling, and operational lifecycle.

4
00:00:21.836 --> 00:00:24.820
Confidentiality alone is usually not enough.

5
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:34.653
If a protected value can be modified without detection, the application may recover data that no longer represents what was originally protected.

6
00:00:34.653 --> 00:00:46.113
Authenticated encryption protects confidentiality and also detects unauthorized modification, which makes it a better fit for many application data protection scenarios.

7
00:00:46.113 --> 00:00:53.844
Nonces and IVs must be generated, used, and stored according to the requirements of the chosen algorithm and mode.

8
00:00:53.844 --> 00:00:56.014
Some designs require uniqueness.

9
00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:58.048
Some require unpredictability.

10
00:00:58.048 --> 00:00:59.405
Some require both.

11
00:00:59.405 --> 00:01:08.356
Reusing a nonce with certain algorithms can undermine the intended protection, so algorithm-specific rules must be followed exactly.

12
00:01:08.356 --> 00:01:16.155
Developers should prefer maintained authenticated-encryption APIs or framework services when they fit the use case.

13
00:01:16.155 --> 00:01:17.511
If ASP.

14
00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:25.920
NET Core Data Protection fits the protected-payload scenario, it is often a better choice than hand-writing encryption code.

15
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:34.193
If direct encryption is required, the team should document the chosen API, key source, nonce handling, and storage format.

16
00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:40.025
Avoid hand-combining encryption and MAC logic unless implementing a reviewed protocol.

17
00:01:40.025 --> 00:01:47.077
The order of operations, covered data, key separation, error behavior, and compatibility details matter.

18
00:01:47.077 --> 00:01:52.163
A construction that looks reasonable can still miss important requirements.

19
00:01:52.163 --> 00:02:06.608
A safe design record should explain what is encrypted, why confidentiality is needed, how integrity is checked, where the key lives, how nonces or IVs are handled, and how the data can be rotated or retired later.
